bug_descriptions |bug_id, line_num, content|
    seed_jit_hoisted_loop_data_ptr_reaches_generic_array_get_2026-11-27, 0, "Repro (origin seed c6f98def228712ea, release/1.0.0, aarch64, JIT; interpreter correct): fn walk(d: [f64]) -> i64: val n = d.len(); var i: u64 = 0; while i < n: print \"{d[i]}\"; i = i + 1 over [7.5, 7.5] prints 0.000... twice (interpreter 7.5). Same loop over [i32] / [i16] [7, 7] prints 3, over [text] [\"ab\"] prints an empty line; a local array in main behaves the same, so it is not parameter-only. A plain i64 counter (var i = 0) is not hoisted and reads correctly. Root: loop_len_bound_proof (src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/mir/lower/lowering_core.rs) admits any array element type, the stmt lowering hoists rt_array_data_ptr once per loop (lowering_stmt.rs ~1730, since 2026-11-27 the word pointer carries bit 0 = byte-packed), and lower_index_expr (lowering_expr_struct.rs:369) replaces receiver_reg with that raw data pointer. Only the [u8]/[u32]/[u64]/[i64] arms know about it (the i64 arm re-lowers the receiver); the generic fallbacks rt_array_get / rt_array_get_text (~line 636) and rt_index_get (~line 692) receive the raw data pointer as if it were the array handle and read garbage silently. Fix direction: re-lower the receiver for every non-word arm (as the i64 arm does), or restrict the hoist to element types whose arm consumes the data pointer. Pre-existing; found in review of e0fab4aeaf6 (Fable)."

